1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,714
Episode 23.

3
00:00:04,714 --> 00:00:07,527
The Shadow That Changed Its Mind.

4
00:00:07,527 --> 00:00:10,244
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,244 --> 00:00:13,709
Did the pole need to move for its shadow to change?

6
00:00:13,709 --> 00:00:15,135
Ready?

7
00:00:15,135 --> 00:00:17,214
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,214 --> 00:00:22,146
At nine in the morning, Maya marks the end of a pole's shadow with chalk.

9
00:00:22,146 --> 00:00:25,258
Leo writes the time beside the mark.

10
00:00:25,258 --> 00:00:28,016
At lunchtime, they return.

11
00:00:28,016 --> 00:00:31,100
The shadow has moved and changed length.

12
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:35,557
The pole is walking when nobody watches, Pip suggests.

13
00:00:35,557 --> 00:00:37,975
Maya checks its base.

14
00:00:37,975 --> 00:00:41,032
The pole has stayed in the same place.

15
00:00:41,032 --> 00:00:45,244
They return once more in the afternoon and add another mark.

16
00:00:45,244 --> 00:00:49,483
Now their three observations tell a small story about the day.

17
00:00:49,483 --> 00:00:54,130
Ms. Park explains that a shadow forms when something blocks light.

18
00:00:54,130 --> 00:00:58,695
The Sun's apparent position in the sky changes as Earth rotates.

19
00:00:58,695 --> 00:01:02,214
That changes the direction and length of the shadow.

20
00:01:02,214 --> 00:01:06,426
In the classroom, they use a flashlight and a toy figure.

21
00:01:06,426 --> 00:01:10,448
When the light is low, the figure makes a long shadow.

22
00:01:10,448 --> 00:01:14,239
When the light is higher, the shadow becomes shorter.

23
00:01:14,239 --> 00:01:17,540
Leo places a tiny hat on the figure.

24
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:20,366
The shadow gets a hat, too.

25
00:01:20,366 --> 00:01:22,323
Pip is delighted.

26
00:01:22,323 --> 00:01:25,828
A complete fashion show with only one model.

27
00:01:25,828 --> 00:01:31,779
Maya notices that the experiment is useful but not a perfect copy of the Sun and Earth.

28
00:01:31,779 --> 00:01:35,271
Their flashlight is much closer than the Sun.

29
00:01:35,271 --> 00:01:39,442
They place the day's marks on a drawing and label the times.

30
00:01:39,442 --> 00:01:42,173
Could we use shadows to tell time?

31
00:01:42,173 --> 00:01:44,116
Leo asks.

32
00:01:44,116 --> 00:01:48,152
People have done that with sundials, says Ms. Park.

33
00:01:48,152 --> 00:01:53,152
Pip adds one condition: The clock may take a break on a cloudy day.

34
00:01:53,152 --> 00:01:56,059
Now, a little more to discover.

35
00:01:56,059 --> 00:01:59,619
A shadow appears where an object blocks light.

36
00:01:59,619 --> 00:02:05,489
Its size and direction depend on the arrangement of the light, object, and surface.

37
00:02:05,489 --> 00:02:09,728
Earth's rotation makes the Sun appear to move across the sky.

38
00:02:09,728 --> 00:02:15,067
Sundials use changing shadows to indicate time when there is suitable sunlight.

39
00:02:15,067 --> 00:02:17,907
Let us hear the main idea again.

40
00:02:17,907 --> 00:02:22,241
The pole stayed still, but its shadow changed during the day.

41
00:02:22,241 --> 00:02:26,603
A flashlight model showed how light direction affects a shadow.

42
00:02:26,603 --> 00:02:32,010
The club used time labels to turn separate observations into a useful record.

43
00:02:32,010 --> 00:02:34,986
Here are three useful expressions.

44
00:02:34,986 --> 00:02:38,111
You can listen, or say them with me.

45
00:02:38,111 --> 00:02:40,543
It is shorter than before.

46
00:02:40,543 --> 00:02:42,975
It is shorter than before.

47
00:02:42,975 --> 00:02:45,421
We measured it in the morning.

48
00:02:45,421 --> 00:02:47,866
We measured it in the morning.

49
00:02:47,866 --> 00:02:49,959
What stayed the same?

50
00:02:49,959 --> 00:02:52,051
What stayed the same?

51
00:02:52,051 --> 00:02:54,646
Remember our listening question?

52
00:02:54,646 --> 00:02:58,111
Did the pole need to move for its shadow to change?

53
00:02:58,111 --> 00:03:00,244
Here is the answer.

54
00:03:00,244 --> 00:03:01,725
No.

55
00:03:01,725 --> 00:03:08,274
The direction of the sunlight changed as Earth rotated, so the shadow changed while the pole stayed still.

56
00:03:08,274 --> 00:03:11,929
And here is a question just for your imagination.

57
00:03:11,929 --> 00:03:17,445
What would you need to keep the same if you wanted to compare one shadow at different times?

58
00:03:17,445 --> 00:03:20,557
There is more than one possible answer.

59
00:03:20,557 --> 00:03:23,804
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
